  "translation": {
    "literal": "Fine grape wine in cups that glow at night; I am about to drink when the pipa urges us on from horseback. Do not laugh if I lie drunk on the sandy field, my lord: since ancient times, how many men have come back from campaigns?",
    "poetic": "Grape wine in a cup of glowing jade; I raise it to drink and the pipa urges us on from horseback. Do not laugh if I lie drunk on the sand, for how many have come back from war since time began?"
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "title",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "A song title",
      "body": "涼州詞 is not a poem title but a song tune, named after Liangzhou (modern Wuwei, Gansu), a frontier garrison town on the Silk Road. Many Tang poets wrote words for this tune. Wang Han (c. 687–726) wrote the best known."
    },
    {
      "id": "mood",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "Bold or bitter?",
      "body": "Readers disagree about this poem's mood. One view is that it is a reckless drinking song of men who accept death. Another is that it is a bitter protest against endless war. The last line allows both: the soldiers drink, joke and laugh at death because they know how few come home."
    },
    {
      "id": "cup",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "The luminous night cup",
      "body": "夜光杯 are cups carved from a pale green jade-like stone from the Qilian Mountains, said in legend to glow in the moonlight. 琵琶 (pipa) came to China from Central Asia, so the whole opening has a foreign, frontier feel."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 杯 · 催 · 回",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 杯 (bēi), 催 (cuī) and 回 (huí), all in the 灰 rhyme. Line three ends in 笑 and does not rhyme."
    }
  ],
